Falsifies `page_frames::verification::two_allocations_are_distinct`.

The defect: the bit is written with the wrong polarity, `self.set(i, false)` in place of
`self.set(i, true)`, so allocating a frame marks it free. `set` takes a `bool` named `used` and
nothing in the type system distinguishes the two calls; the freeing path a few lines away passes
`false` correctly.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_ne!(fa.addr(), fb.addr())`. The hint makes the second
scan start past the frame just handed out, so the two allocations still differ whenever another
frame is free; the counterexample is a bitmap where the frame allocated is the only free one and
the wrapped scan hands it straight back. That is the double-allocation address-space isolation
rests on.

diff --git a/crates/page_frames/src/lib.rs b/crates/page_frames/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/page_frames/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/page_frames/src/lib.rs
@@ -257,3 +257,3 @@
 
-        self.set(i, true);
+        self.set(i, false);
         self.used += 1;
